1 0 0 1 50 203.89 Tm (The navigation grid cannot be modelled merely as a static 2D planar graph because the) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 189.89 Tm (connectivity of the environment changes dynamically as obstacles are destroyed, and) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 175.89 Tm (the legality of moves depends on available energy. We model the problem as a) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 161.89 Tm (**Weighted Directed State-Space Graph** $\\mathcal{G} = \(\\mathcal{S}, \\mathcal{A},) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 147.89 Tm (c\)$:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 133.89 Tm (* $\\mathcal{S}$: The set of all reachable robot states.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 119.88999999999999 Tm (* $\\mathcal{A}$: Directed transitions between valid states representing a move or a) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 105.88999999999999 Tm (destruction followed by a move.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 91.88999999999999 Tm (* $c$: The cost function $c: \\mathcal{A} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$, which can take) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 77.88999999999999 Tm (positive, zero, or negative values.) Tj
