1 0 0 1 50 791.89 Tm ($G$ such that Kruskals algorithm, when processing edges in this order, outputs) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 777.89 Tm (exactly $T$.*) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 763.89 Tm () Tj
1 0 0 1 50 749.89 Tm (#### Formal Inductive Proof) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 735.89 Tm (1. **Partition by Weight:**) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 721.89 Tm (   Let the distinct edge weights in $G$ be $w_1 < w_2 < \\dots < w_k$.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 707.89 Tm (   Partition the entire edge set $E$ into equivalence classes by weight:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 693.89 Tm (   $$E_i = \\{e \\in E \\mid w\(e\) = w_i\\}$$) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 679.89 Tm (   Similarly, let $T_i = T \\cap E_i$ be the set of edges in $T$ having weight $w_i$.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 665.89 Tm () Tj
1 0 0 1 50 651.89 Tm (2. **Construction of Ordering $\\sigma$:**) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 637.89 Tm (   Construct a total ordering $\\sigma$ of $E$ adhering to non-decreasing weights:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 623.89 Tm (   * For each weight class $E_i$, arrange the edges such that all edges belonging to) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 609.89 Tm ($T_i$ appear **strictly before** all edges in $E_i \\setminus T_i$.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 595.89 Tm (   * Concatenate these ordered classes:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 581.89 Tm (     $$\\sigma = \\big\( \\text{edges in } T_1, \\; E_1 \\setminus T_1, \\; \\text{edges in }) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 567.89 Tm (T_2, \\; E_2 \\setminus T_2, \\; \\dots, \\; \\text{edges in } T_k, \\; E_k \\setminus T_k) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 553.89 Tm (\\big\)$$) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 539.89 Tm () Tj
1 0 0 1 50 525.89 Tm (3. **Inductive Hypothesis:**) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 511.89 Tm (   *Claim:* After Kruskals algorithm finishes processing all edges of weight $\\le) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 497.89 Tm (w_i$, the set of edges accepted by Kruskal is exactly:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 483.89 Tm (   $$\\bigcup_{j=1}^i T_j$$) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 469.89 Tm () Tj
1 0 0 1 50 455.89 Tm (4. **Base Case \($i = 0$\):**) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 441.89 Tm (   Before processing any edges, Kruskals accepted edge set is $\\emptyset$, matching) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 427.89 Tm (the base set.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 413.89 Tm () Tj
1 0 0 1 50 399.89 Tm (5. **Inductive Step:**) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 385.89 Tm (   Assume the claim holds for weight class $w_{i-1}$. That is, the current forest) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 371.89 Tm (maintained by the Disjoint-Set Union \(DSU\) structure consists exactly of the edges) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 357.89 Tm ($T_1 \\cup \\dots \\cup T_{i-1}$.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 343.89 Tm (   Now Kruskal processes edges of weight $w_i$:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 329.89 Tm (   ) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 315.89 Tm (   * **Phase A \(Edges in $T_i$\):**) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 301.89 Tm (     Let $e = \(u, v\) \\in T_i$. ) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 287.89 Tm (     Suppose for contradiction that $u$ and $v$ are already in the same connected) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 273.89 Tm (component of the DSU. That would imply there exists a path between $u$ and $v$ using) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 259.89 Tm (only edges from $T_1 \\cup \\dots \\cup T_{i-1} \\subseteq T$. But adding $e \\in T$ would) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 245.89 Tm (form a cycle in $T$, violating the fact that $T$ is a tree.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 231.89 Tm (     Therefore, $u$ and $v$ must belong to distinct components:) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 217.89 Tm (     $$\\text{DSU.find}\(u\) \\neq \\text{DSU.find}\(v\)$$) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 203.89 Tm (     Hence, Kruskal **must accept** every edge $e \\in T_i$.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 189.89 Tm () Tj
1 0 0 1 50 175.89 Tm (   * **Phase B \(Edges in $E_i \\setminus T_i$\):**) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 161.89 Tm (     Let $e' = \(x, y\) \\in E_i \\setminus T_i$.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 147.89 Tm (     Since $T$ is a spanning tree, adding $e'$ to $T$ produces a unique fundamental) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 133.89 Tm (cycle $C$.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 119.88999999999999 Tm (     Because $T$ is an MST, no edge on $C$ can have a weight strictly greater than) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 105.88999999999999 Tm ($w\(e'\) = w_i$ \(by the Cycle Property of MSTs\).) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 91.88999999999999 Tm (     Thus, all edges on cycle $C$ belong to $T$ and have weight $\\le w_i$.) Tj
1 0 0 1 50 77.88999999999999 Tm (     All such edges \($T_1 \\cup \\dots \\cup T_i$\) have already been accepted by Kruskal) Tj
ET

